_A one-line description of {{project_name}} goes here._

**Start with the documentation tree.** For project context, conventions, architecture, and per-subsystem deep-dives, read [`docs/README.md`](/docs/README.md) first. It is the canonical source of truth for how the system fits together. The guidelines below are *how* to work on the project; the docs tree explains *what* exists.

## Conventions

<!-- /bootstrap fills this from the brief and the repo's stack. -->

_The coding conventions for this project — language idioms, style, structure, naming, error handling, and anything the tooling enforces. Keep these aligned with the `code-conventions.md` doc and with what the `[capabilities]` in `.icculus/config.toml` actually check, so the written rule and the enforced rule never disagree._

## Keeping documentation updated

The [`docs/`](/docs/README.md) tree is the canonical source of truth and describes only what currently exists in code, so it must not drift. When a change meaningfully alters something the docs describe — the architecture, the data model, a subsystem's documented behaviour, a public convention, or whether a feature exists — make the necessary documentation updates before you finish the task. Use the [`document-subsystem`](/.icculus/skills/document-subsystem/SKILL.md) skill to write or refresh a subtree, and [`write-adr`](/.icculus/skills/write-adr/SKILL.md) to record a significant decision.

## Tracking open work

When you defer a fix, descope, or find a real issue you will not address this round, record it in the root [`TODO.md`](/TODO.md) — the shared backlog every agent and the maintainer can see. Do not leave deferred work only in a code comment or a session note. `TODO.md` documents its own format; `docs/` stays present-tense (what exists), while `TODO.md` holds what is outstanding.

## Working in a worktree

Sessions usually run inside a linked git worktree with its own checkout and config. Stay inside the worktree root: every path you construct and every directory change must stay within it — never read, edit, or write files in the main checkout from a worktree session. When the work is ready to hand back, graduate the branch into the main checkout with `icculus worktree:exit` (the [`handoff-worktree`](/.icculus/skills/handoff-worktree/SKILL.md) skill drives this).

## Finishing a task

After any coding task, run **`icculus finish`** before presenting your work as done. It is the complete end-to-end gate — it applies your fixers, builds, runs static checks and tests in parallel, fires any scope `gate`s that fired, and (in a worktree) verifies your branch contains the latest `main`. If it reports anything, fix it and run it again; do not declare a task done on a red gate. `icculus tidy` is the faster inner loop (fixers + checks, no build or tests) for use while iterating.

When `icculus finish` stops at the merge check, it means `main` advanced during your session: commit your work, run `git merge main` (resolve conflicts, commit), then run `icculus finish` again. If a stage fails in a way the message does not explain, the gate points you at [`docs/80-development/finish-gate-gotchas.md`](/docs/80-development/finish-gate-gotchas.md) — read the matching entry, and add a new one if you hit a trap that is not yet recorded.
